WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.160 --> 00:00:03.760
We all know that cats are the&nbsp;
true epitome of chaotic neutral.

00:00:03.760 --> 00:00:06.000
In a single breath, the internet’s favorite fuzzy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:09.520
creatures go from cuddling against&nbsp;
your chest to clawing at your face.

00:00:10.240 --> 00:00:14.640
Millions of people find their pouncing,&nbsp;
purring and prowling fascinating - others&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.640 --> 00:00:19.200
find it downright unnerving And it’s not just&nbsp;
the modern world that’s obsessed with cats.&nbsp;

00:00:19.200 --> 00:00:22.000
Not quite deities, and definitely not human,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.000 --> 00:00:26.240
cats have stalked our cultural&nbsp;
imagination since ancient times.

00:00:26.240 --> 00:00:30.320
As demons, defenders, evil&nbsp;
sidekicks, and sleek companions,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:30.320 --> 00:00:34.640
they play many roles in culture and&nbsp;
myth. But what makes cats so enduring,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.640 --> 00:00:37.840
and what does our feline fascination&nbsp;
tell us about human nature?&nbsp;

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:46.320
With cat references scattered all over the&nbsp;
ancient world, it’s impossible to determine&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:46.320 --> 00:00:51.680
a single origin point for our obsession.&nbsp;
Prehistoric art reveals some striking felines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:54.800
from a painted red lion gracing&nbsp;
the Pech Merle cave in France,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:54.800 --> 00:00:59.200
to 7000-year-old stone leopards&nbsp;
circling the temple of Uvda in Israel.&nbsp;

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:02.960
Other depictions show some&nbsp;
unexpected cat-human contact.

00:01:02.960 --> 00:01:08.960
At the settlement of Çatalhöyük in Anatolia,&nbsp;
which thrived around 7000BCE, a woman is shown&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.960 --> 00:01:13.520
in a birthing position - her hands resting&nbsp;
on the backs of leopards for support.

00:01:13.520 --> 00:01:16.560
Another nearby site , has yielded&nbsp;
figurines of pregnant women&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.560 --> 00:01:22.000
and new mothers nursing leopards.
These examples draw a direct line between cats and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:26.320
mighty motherhood. And while it’s still debated&nbsp;
whether the mother-cats were deities, there’s a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:26.320 --> 00:01:32.240
cat woman lurking in pretty much every pantheon. Some goddesses take cats as their co-conspirators,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.240 --> 00:01:38.000
like the Hindu figure of Durga. Riding on her&nbsp;
lion, Durga is worshiped as both nurturing mother&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:38.000 --> 00:01:43.360
and menacing warrior. Flanked by lions,&nbsp;
the Roman and Anatolian goddess Cybele&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.360 --> 00:01:48.400
also guards the victims of war - while reserving&nbsp;
the right to inflict some serious harm.&nbsp;

00:01:48.400 --> 00:01:53.040
The Norse goddess of love, Freya, rode&nbsp;
in a chariot pulled by giant cats.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.040 --> 00:01:56.880
Her trusty steeds are suggestive&nbsp;
of Freya’s grace and affection.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:56.880 --> 00:02:02.560
But she could also turn fearsome as the leader of&nbsp;
the all-female band of warriors, the Valkyries.&nbsp;

00:02:02.560 --> 00:02:06.240
But not all feline protectors&nbsp;
are tied to femininity.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.240 --> 00:02:12.480
Chinese guardian lions come in male and female&nbsp;
form and have long guarded Buddhist temples.

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:17.200
Assuming either a smile or a snarl,&nbsp;
these lions are agents of righteousness&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:17.200 --> 00:02:22.640
that you definitely don’t want to cross.
These figures all embody the paradoxical idea that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:22.640 --> 00:02:28.240
tenderness and destruction are two sides of the&nbsp;
same coin. Like their divine counterparts, cats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.240 --> 00:02:34.560
could be the object of both admiration and fear –&nbsp;
associated with the most formidable forces even as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:34.560 --> 00:02:38.880
they were gradually welcomed into the home.
For a key example of how cats straddled the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.880 --> 00:02:44.000
domestic and divine, we can turn to Ancient&nbsp;
Egypt. The Ancient Egyptians may be the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.000 --> 00:02:49.840
committed cat people of all time - but this&nbsp;
love was rooted in a degree of practicality.

00:02:49.840 --> 00:02:52.640
Between 4000 and 2000 BCE,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.640 --> 00:02:58.480
more and more grain was stored in Egyptian&nbsp;
towns – drawing rodents and, ultimately, cats.&nbsp;

00:02:59.200 --> 00:03:03.280
Far beyond pest control, cats came&nbsp;
to be worshiped in their own right.

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:07.360
An entire branch of government&nbsp;
was dedicated to their protection,

00:03:07.360 --> 00:03:10.480
and any outsider who harmed&nbsp;
a cat was sentenced to death.&nbsp;

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:16.720
Archaeological digs have recovered thousands&nbsp;
of mummified cats, amulets and statues,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:21.200
as well as cemeteries filled with pets who&nbsp;
accompanied their owners to the afterlife.

00:03:21.200 --> 00:03:25.680
They were often depicted on the laps of the dead&nbsp;
– but just in case you thought humans were in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.680 --> 00:03:31.680
control here, cats could also be found terrorizing&nbsp;
the underworld and presiding over human life.

00:03:31.680 --> 00:03:35.040
In addition to their animal&nbsp;
forms cats were theophanies,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.040 --> 00:03:38.240
or IRL manifestations of the Egyptian gods.

00:03:38.240 --> 00:03:40.880
With the body of a woman and the face of a lion,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.880 --> 00:03:43.440
Sekhmet destroyed&nbsp;
the enemies of Ancient Egypt.&nbsp;

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:49.520
Often crowned with a sun disk, she burned her foes&nbsp;
to the ground and could spread war and plagues.

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:53.600
At the same time, she was honored&nbsp;
as a great physician and was thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.600 --> 00:03:58.880
to oversee the conception of the Pharaohs.
On the other hand, Bastet, another cat goddess&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.880 --> 00:04:04.400
who also had the body of a woman but the face of&nbsp;
a cat, was more of a cat goddess of the people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:04.400 --> 00:04:09.200
associated with childbirth, music, and&nbsp;
dance. Like Sekhmet, she was linked to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:09.200 --> 00:04:14.160
the sun – but more in terms of warmth and&nbsp;
safety, rather than blazing destruction.&nbsp;

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:18.480
While every cat loves a stint in the sun,&nbsp;
the Greek goddess Artemis worked by the light&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.480 --> 00:04:23.840
of the moon. Occasionally depicted in cat&nbsp;
form, Artemis was the goddess of hunting,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.840 --> 00:04:28.880
animals, and childbirth – who could swiftly&nbsp;
slay any living thing with her golden arrows.&nbsp;

00:04:28.880 --> 00:04:32.880
As predators and protectors who could&nbsp;
be associated with darkness and light,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:36.880
these mythical figures concretize&nbsp;
the dual nature of the feline.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:36.880 --> 00:04:42.640
For many, this ambivalence was a source of awe and&nbsp;
fascination. For others, it raised some hackles.&nbsp;

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:48.880
Many mythologies frame cats as a threat rather&nbsp;
than an asset. In Persian Zoroastrian mythology,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.880 --> 00:04:52.480
cats were created by the Evil Spirit&nbsp;
and believed to spread treachery.

00:04:53.040 --> 00:04:57.760
Some of the most sinister Japanese yōkai,&nbsp;
or supernatural beings, are also cats.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:01.600
In one tale, a young man named Takasu&nbsp;
Genbei had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.600 --> 00:05:06.080
a cat who vanished – just as his&nbsp;
mother retreated from family life.

00:05:06.080 --> 00:05:10.000
When he finally pried open her&nbsp;
bedroom door, Takasu saw not his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:10.000 --> 00:05:13.600
mother, but a monstrous feline&nbsp;
demon curled in her clothes.

00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:18.960
He aimed to save her by slaying the beast, but&nbsp;
the carcass only morphed into his missing cat.

00:05:19.520 --> 00:05:22.880
Weeks later, he found his mother’s&nbsp;
bones under the floorboards.&nbsp;

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:26.720
Takasu’s mother was plagued&nbsp;
by a yōkai known as bakeneko,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.720 --> 00:05:30.000
demons who devour their owners&nbsp;
and inhabit their body.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:33.360
Bakeneko are formed when an&nbsp;
ordinary cat drinks blood,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.360 --> 00:05:37.280
licks too much lamp oil, or even&nbsp;
if their tail becomes too long.

00:05:37.280 --> 00:05:44.160
The bakeneko can become another feline yokai, the&nbsp;
two-tailed nekomata which is even more dangerous,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.160 --> 00:05:49.680
growing to enormous sizes and hurling fireballs&nbsp;
at humans when it’s not busy devouring them.

00:05:50.320 --> 00:05:54.800
As malevolent cats, these yokai represent&nbsp;
an enduring fear of the unknown–&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.800 --> 00:06:00.240
and what happens when we let it into our homes.
Ancient Greeks were also plagued by invasive cats,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:00.240 --> 00:06:02.320
most notably the Nemean lion.

00:06:02.320 --> 00:06:05.280
The offspring of monsters&nbsp;
with an impenetrable hide,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.280 --> 00:06:10.320
razor-sharp teeth, and a taste for human&nbsp;
flesh, the lion was only defeated when Hercules&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:15.680
barricaded it in its cave and strangled it with&nbsp;
his bare hands - you know, because he’s extra.

00:06:15.680 --> 00:06:20.960
He wore the skin of the lion thereafter, where it&nbsp;
remained a terrifying sign of his outsized power.&nbsp;

00:06:20.960 --> 00:06:24.640
When Hera learned that her husband&nbsp;
Zues had impregnated Alcmene&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.640 --> 00:06:28.240
she sent the goddess of childbirth&nbsp;
Eileithyia to delay the birth.

00:06:28.240 --> 00:06:33.840
Alcmene’s midwife, Galinthias, managed to distract&nbsp;
Eileithyia while her child Hercules was born.

00:06:33.840 --> 00:06:39.680
Hera was so furious that she turned Galinthias&nbsp;
into a cat and banished her to the underworld.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.680 --> 00:06:44.160
There she became the companion of the&nbsp;
goddess of magic and discord, Hecate.&nbsp;

00:06:44.160 --> 00:06:46.880
With the rise of Christianity, this image of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.880 --> 00:06:52.480
unpredictable woman with her cat infused&nbsp;
fears of paganism and so-called dark magic.

00:06:52.480 --> 00:06:54.880
The suspicion of cats endured for centuries.

00:06:54.880 --> 00:07:00.240
From Pope Gregory IX denouncing cats and&nbsp;
black cats specifically as agents of Satan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.240 --> 00:07:04.560
in the thirteenth century, to the rise of&nbsp;
witch hunts in the Middle Ages and beyond.&nbsp;

00:07:04.560 --> 00:07:10.800
In 1658, the cleric Edward Topsel wrote that “the&nbsp;
familiars of Witches do most ordinarily appear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:16.320
in the shape of Cats, which is an argument that&nbsp;
this beast is dangerous to soul and body.”

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:22.000
At a time of high paranoia, woe betide any&nbsp;
single woman who happened to be a cat person.&nbsp;

00:07:22.000 --> 00:07:26.400
Across different cultures, cats give&nbsp;
shape to some of our most primal fears,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.400 --> 00:07:30.800
perhaps the most famous example being&nbsp;
black cats’ association with bad luck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:30.800 --> 00:07:35.360
evil omens and the possibility that&nbsp;
an unknown enemy lurks in our midst.&nbsp;

00:07:35.360 --> 00:07:39.840
On the other hand, some myths suggest that&nbsp;
welcoming the unknown can be a good thing.

00:07:39.840 --> 00:07:45.360
In the early seventeenth century, a cat invited&nbsp;
the Japanese lord Ii Naotaka to shelter in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.360 --> 00:07:51.520
Buddhist temple of Gotokuji. In gratitude, Ii&nbsp;
Naotaka dedicated the temple to all cats. 
 &nbsp;

00:07:51.520 --> 00:07:56.560
This legend is said to be the birth of the&nbsp;
maneki-neko, or Japanese beckoning cat.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.560 --> 00:08:00.480
Widely seen clocking their paws in&nbsp;
temples, shrines, businesses, and homes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.480 --> 00:08:06.400
the beckoning cat is a feline of good fortune.
As predator and prey, trickster and tormentor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.400 --> 00:08:10.880
mythical cats can rarely be reduced&nbsp;
to pure good or absolute evil.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.880 --> 00:08:16.320
It’s telling, then, that cats are often associated&nbsp;
with liminal spaces – particularly the ghostly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.320 --> 00:08:21.520
line that separates the living from the dead.&nbsp;
Depending on who you ask, cats can help or&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:21.520 --> 00:08:27.120
hinder the journey of souls to the next life.
Certain Buddhist sects believe that the soul is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.120 --> 00:08:31.680
transferred to a cat for safekeeping,while&nbsp;
in Finnish folklore cats were tasked with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.680 --> 00:08:37.040
transporting the souls of the dead to the&nbsp;
underworld. Other mythologies are less trusting.&nbsp;

00:08:37.040 --> 00:08:42.880
The Celtic creature Cáit Sith appears as a&nbsp;
black cat with white markings on its chest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.880 --> 00:08:47.600
and tends to sneak up on mourners and steal&nbsp;
their beloved’s soul. And then there’s the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:47.600 --> 00:08:52.160
Japanese Kasha, demonic corpse-catcher…but&nbsp;
you can look to Monstrum for that one.&nbsp;

00:08:52.160 --> 00:08:57.680
Today you can find a cat in more domestic liminal&nbsp;
spaces - camping out in sock drawers, squeezing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:57.680 --> 00:09:01.840
through doorways, and curling up in that space&nbsp;
between shelf and floor that never quite gets&nbsp;&nbsp;clear. 

00:09:01.840 --> 00:09:07.280
Even as they’ve become stalwarts of our&nbsp;
homes, there’s always something bewildering about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:13.840
these creatures and their stare can be… haunting.
Centuries on, we struggle with the same questions&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.840 --> 00:09:19.552
as our ancestors: Are cats fully domesticated,&nbsp;
why are they looking at us like that, and what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.552 --> 00:09:25.200
is that sound they’re making? In the end, the&nbsp;
intrigue of cats stems from their mystery – and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.200 --> 00:09:30.002
while we’re no closer to solving it, it’s&nbsp;
clear that the feline imaginary can’t be tamed.

